                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Anchorage AK",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 28 at 11:34AM AKDT until October 30 at 4:00PM AKDT by NWS Anchorage AK",
                "description": "* WHAT...Small stream and localized urban flooding caused by\nexcessive rainfall is possible.\n\n* WHERE...Seward.\n\n* WHEN...From Thursday evening through Friday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive rainfall may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\nStorm drains and ditches may become clogged with debris. Flooding\nmay occur in poor drainage areas.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- A strong frontal system with an atmospheric river of moisture\nwill move over the southern Kenai Peninsula Thursday through\nFriday. Although light rain will begin Thursday morning, a period\nof heavy rain is expected late Thursday evening through Friday\nmorning before the rain slowly tapers off Friday afternoon.\nRainfall rates of up to a quarter of an inch per hour are\npossible between 10 PM Thursday and 10 AM Friday, with a storm\ntotal between 3 and 4 inches of rain likely with event. Prolonged\nheavy rain may lead to areas of flooding along smaller streams\nand creeks, in low-lying and poor drainage areas, and ponding of\nwater on roadways.\n\n- Http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "Stay tuned to further developments by listening to your local radio,\ntelevision, or NOAA Weather Radio for further information.\n\nYou should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.\n\nFlooding may occur in poor drainage areas.\n\nStorm drains should be kept clear of debris as much as possible.",
                "response": "Prepare",
